 "finished_by_agent": true,
 "finish_summary": "I replaced the 32-deep ternary priority chain with a logarithmic suffix-OR (Kogge-Stone style) formulation: m[k]=|a[31:k] computed in 5 parallel OR stages, with each index bit derived by telescoping identities (e.g. idx[4]=m[16], idx[3]=m[24]|(m[8]&~m[16]), ... idx[0] = OR of 16 (m[2k+1]&~m[2k+2]) terms), and hit = m[0] so that y_o={hit,idx} is naturally 0 when a_i=0. The register stage was rewritten as a single merged enable/sync-reset gate (y_o <= ({6{rst_n&valid_i}}&chosen) | ({6{rst_n&~valid_i}}&y_o)), saving a logic level and cells. Probing with cheap estimates revealed that (a) only ~0.13 ns of the 2.35 ns path is combinational logic (a bare 32-input OR alone costs ~0.10 ns), (b) moving logic after the flop is worse (output path is tighter), and (c) ABC's result is very sensitive to the written tree shape \u2014 explicitly balancing the wide OR expressions into binary trees gained +0.02 ns estimated and was the single biggest win, bringing the design to the measured pure-OR timing floor. Several alternatives (group-of-4 encoders, pair-level prefix, mux tree, radix-4 prefix, other prefix stage orders, input registering) were all measured worse and rejected before spending P&R budget. Final routed result: area 212.8 um^2, WNS 7.67838 ns, score 76.571 (+0.324 over the 76.247 baseline).",
